<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ page_direction }}">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ title }}{% if english %} · {{ english }}{% endif %}{% if not standalone %} — {{ section.title }}{% endif %}</title>
{% include '_icons.html.j2' %}
<style>{{ asset('reader.css') }}</style>
{{ hebrew_face(biblical) }}
<script>{{ asset('durable.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('theme.js') }}</script>
</head>
<body class="mode-parallel{% if words %} has-list{% endif %}{% if mark_guessed %} mark-guessed{% endif %}{% if verse_by_verse %} verses{% endif %}">
<header class="bar">
  {#- The mark holds the corner on its own: at this size the identity is the monogram,
      not the lockup, and the bar already carries the title beside it.

      It is also the way back. A mark in the corner going home is the oldest convention
      on the web, and it was sitting inert next to a link that said "Learn" — the same
      journey, twice, in the narrowest part of the bar. `id="home"` moves onto it, so the
      reader wires it exactly as before: a link only where there is somewhere to go, and
      carrying the key it was served with. -#}
  <a class="bar-brand" id="home" href="/" title="Your Learn page" aria-label="targum — Learn" hidden>
    <svg viewBox="20 12 56 72" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <rect x="20" y="12" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="currentColor"></rect>
      <rect x="54" y="22" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="var(--mark-accent)"></rect>
    </svg>
  </a>
  {#- Off the disk there is no Learn page, so the mark is a mark rather than a dead
      link. Same drawing, no href. -#}
  <span class="bar-brand" id="home-plain" role="img" aria-label="targum">
    <svg viewBox="20 12 56 72" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <rect x="20" y="12" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="currentColor"></rect>
      <rect x="54" y="22" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="var(--mark-accent)"></rect>
    </svg>
  </span>
  <div class="bar-title">
    {% if not standalone %}<a class="up" href="index.html">{{ title }}</a>{% else %}<span>{{ title }}</span>{% endif %}
    {#- The English beside the Hebrew, its own element so the two directions never share
        a string; gone under 40rem, where the bar has no room, while the document title
        keeps both. -#}
    {% if english %}<span class="bar-english" lang="en" dir="ltr">{{ english }}</span>{% endif %}
    {#- How much of this text you can already read. Filled in by the reader from the
        words it has, and hidden outright on a text that carries none. -#}
    <span class="known" id="known" hidden></span>
  </div>
  <div class="controls" dir="ltr">
    <div class="group modes" id="modes" role="group" aria-label="Reading mode">
      <span class="slide" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <button type="button" data-mode="parallel" class="on" aria-label="Parallel" title="Translation beside the text (p)">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M2.5 4h4M2.5 8h4M2.5 12h4M9.5 4h4M9.5 8h4M9.5 12h4"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
      <button type="button" data-mode="inter" aria-label="Interlinear" title="Translation under each line (l)">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M2.5 3.5h11M2.5 9.5h11"/>
          <path d="M2.5 6.2h6M2.5 12.2h6" class="faint"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
      <button type="button" data-mode="source" aria-label="Source only" title="The text on its own (o)">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M2.5 4h11M2.5 8h11M2.5 12h11"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    {#- Everything but the modes. On a wide window this wrapper is nothing — its groups
        lay out in the bar as they always have — and under 60rem it is the menu the ⋯
        opens, in the band at the foot with the sheet and the card. Each group names
        itself in `data-what`, which is the row's label there and nothing here. -#}
    {#- Beside the modes rather than behind ⋯: on a phone the vowel points are the one
        thing a reader reaches for mid-sentence. -#}
    {% if has_nikkud %}
    <div class="group" data-what="Vowel points">
      <button type="button" data-nikkud-toggle aria-pressed="false"
              {%- if mark_guessed %} title="Vowel points (n). Where this text left a word bare, targum guessed — about 90% right on modern Hebrew, less on classical. Guessed sentences are marked."
              {%- elif source_pointed %} title="Vowel points, as this text writes them (n)"
              {%- else %} title="Vowel points, guessed by targum — about 90% right on modern Hebrew, less on classical (n)"{% endif %}
              aria-label="Vowel points">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M4 3.5h8M8 3.5v6"/>
          <path d="M5 12.5h0M8 12.5h0M11 12.5h0" class="dots"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    <div class="bar-more" id="more">
      <div class="more-head"><b>This text</b><button type="button" data-more aria-label="Close">×</button></div>
    {% if translations|length > 1 %}
    <label class="group">
      <span class="label">Translation</span>
      <select id="translation">
        {% for t in translations %}<option value="{{ t.id }}">{{ t.name }}</option>{% endfor %}
      </select>
    </label>
    {% endif %}
    {#- One button, not two: it is a thing that is on or off, and two buttons for that
        made a scale out of a switch. The drawing is a letter with its points beneath —
        §7 wants a line diagram of what the thing does, and this is literally it. -#}
    {#- Play the scene, and follow it. Only on a text that has audio, which today means a
        dialogue and nothing else. The drawing is what it does: a play mark, and the lines
        of text it is going to walk down. -#}
    {% if spoken_audio %}
    <div class="group" data-what="Listen to {{ spoken_label }}">
      <button type="button" data-play-scene aria-pressed="false"
              title="Play {{ spoken_label }} and follow along" aria-label="Play {{ spoken_label }}">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M2.5 3 6.5 5.5 2.5 8Z"/>
          <path d="M9 5.5h4.5M2.5 11h11M2.5 13.5h7" class="faint"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {#- Watch the recording. Only on a text whose import kept its pictures, and off
        until pressed: the reader is a reader, not a player (§1) — the picture is
        optional and the player strip stays the transport. The drawing is what it
        does: a screen, with the play mark inside it. -#}
    {% if spoken_video %}
    <div class="group" data-what="Watch the recording">
      <button type="button" data-video aria-pressed="false"
              title="The video" aria-label="The video">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <rect x="2" y="3.5" width="12" height="9" rx="1"/>
          <path d="M6.5 6 10 8 6.5 10Z"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {#- Where the video lives, opened at the line in front of the reader. Only where it
        lives somewhere: an uploaded file has no home — the sidecar is the video and
        possession is the whole of ownership — and a dead "open the original" would be
        a control that lies. Second to the toggle beside it, because the sidecar plays
        on a plane and this does not. No referrer on the way out: the page fetches
        nothing, and hands nothing over either. The drawing is a screen with the arrow
        leaving it. -#}
    {% if spoken_home %}
    <div class="group" data-what="Where it lives">
      <a class="home" data-home href="{{ spoken_home }}" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener"
         title="Open on YouTube at this line" aria-label="Open on YouTube at this line">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M8.5 3.5H3a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v7a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h10a1 1 0 0 0 1-1V8.5"/>
          <path d="M7.5 8.5 13.5 2.5M9.5 2.5h4v4"/>
        </svg>
      </a>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {#- The chanting marks, on scripture that carries them. Behind ⋯ rather than beside
        the modes: the vowels are reached for mid-sentence and these are set once, by
        somebody who knows what they are. The drawing is a letter under the tipcha that
        is the commonest of them. -#}
    {% if has_taamim %}
    <div class="group" data-what="Chanting marks">
      <button type="button" data-taamim-toggle aria-pressed="true"
              title="The chanting marks, as the Masorah wrote them (a)" aria-label="Chanting marks">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M4 12.5h8"/>
          <path d="M8 3.2 6.4 6h3.2Z" class="dots"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {#- Pages, or one long scroll. The drawing is two leaves. -#}
    <div class="group" data-what="Pages, or one long scroll">
      <button type="button" data-paged aria-pressed="false"
              title="Pages, or one long scroll (b)" aria-label="Pages">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <rect x="2" y="3" width="5" height="10" rx="1"/>
          <rect x="9" y="3" width="5" height="10" rx="1"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    {#- Reading, or marking. Off is reading: the page is just a page. Only offered on a
        text that has words to mark — the rest carry no annotation at all. -#}
    {% if words %}
    <div class="group" data-what="Highlight what you have not learned">
      <button type="button" data-marking aria-pressed="false"
              title="Highlight the words you have not learned yet, and clear them as you mark them (m)"
              aria-label="Highlight what you have not learned">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M2.5 3.5h11M2.5 12.5h11"/>
          <rect x="2" y="6.3" width="12" height="3.4" rx="1.2"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    {% endif %}
    {%- if siblings %}
    {#- The same news at another level. One press, and the reader keeps their place in
        the issue rather than being sent back to the library to find the easy one. -#}
    <div class="group levels" role="group" aria-label="Level" data-what="Level">
      {%- for other in siblings %}
      {%- if other.current %}
      <span class="level here" aria-current="page" title="Written for {{ other.figure }}">{{ other.name }}</span>
      {%- else %}
      {#- The same section, not the other level's front. A reader who finds Sport too
          hard wants Sport again in easier Hebrew, not to be put back at the top of the
          issue and made to find their way down to it. -#}
      <a class="level" href="../../{{ other.folder }}/reader/{{ 'index.html' if standalone else section.filename }}" title="Written for {{ other.figure }}">{{ other.name }}</a>
      {%- endif %}
      {%- endfor %}
    </div>
    {%- endif %}
    <div class="group" role="group" aria-label="Type size" data-what="Type">
      <button type="button" data-type="smaller" aria-label="Smaller type">A−</button>
      <button type="button" data-type="larger" aria-label="Larger type">A+</button>
      <button type="button" data-type="looser" aria-label="Line spacing" title="Line spacing">↕</button>
    </div>
    {#- The browser's own full screen, where it has one: the reader alone on the glass.
        `reader.js` hides the row where there is none (Safari on a phone), so the bar
        never offers what the browser cannot do. -#}
    <div class="group" data-what="Full screen" id="fullscreen-group" hidden>
      <button type="button" data-fullscreen aria-pressed="false" title="Full screen (f)" aria-label="Full screen">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M2.5 6V2.5H6M10 2.5h3.5V6M13.5 10v3.5H10M6 13.5H2.5V10"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
    <div class="group" data-what="Keys">
      <button type="button" data-keys aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="keys" aria-label="Keyboard shortcuts" title="Keyboard shortcuts (?)">?</button>
    </div>
    <div class="group" data-what="Theme">
      <button type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-label="Switch theme"></button>
    </div>
    </div>
    <button type="button" class="more" data-more aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="more" aria-label="More">⋯</button>
  </div>
</header>

{#- The sections, straight from the bar.

    The weekly is one long targum, which is right for reading it through and wrong for
    somebody who does not want the politics. A strip under the bar rather than more
    controls inside it: five Hebrew section names would crowd a toolbar that is already
    a mark, a title, three modes, three levels, three type buttons and two toggles — and
    a row of section names under the masthead is what a newspaper does anyway. -#}
{%- if parts %}
<nav class="parts" aria-label="Sections">
  {%- for part in parts %}
  <a href="#" data-to="{{ part.id }}" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ part.title }}</a>
  {%- endfor %}
</nav>
{%- endif %}

{#- What a keystroke just did, for anyone who is listening rather than looking. Marking a
    word moves a ring and a number in the far corner; neither is announced, and a reader
    working the queue by keyboard alone got no confirmation of anything at all. -#}
<p class="spoken" id="spoken" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>

{#- The first time, and only the first time: one line that says what to do, and after
    the first word is marked, one line that says what the keys are. A reader who has
    never marked a word in this language sees it; after that it is gone for good. -#}
{#- `dir="ltr"`: the line is English chrome inside a page that takes the text's
    direction, and on an RTL page the bidi algorithm carried its full stop to the
    other end — ".Tap a word to say how well you know it". -#}
{% if words %}<p class="first" id="first" dir="ltr" hidden>Tap a word to say how well you know it.</p>{% endif %}

<main id="reader">
{#- Small on purpose. A chapter page is for reading, and an image that competed with the
    text would be a decoration; this is a mark, the size of a stamp, that says which book
    you are in. Its own cover where the chapter has one, the book's where it has not. -#}
{% if plate %}<figure class="plate"><img src="{{ plate }}" alt=""></figure>{% endif %}
{#- A chapter nobody has paid for yet. It says so, once, above the text, and offers to
    get it; the source is still there to read and mark. The translation column is left
    out rather than shipped empty — a column of blank paragraphs said nothing about
    why, and read as a page that had broken. -#}
{% if not translated and not standalone %}
<div class="waiting-note" id="waiting-note" data-chapter="{{ section.number }}">
  {#- An imported recording's part that nobody has bought is waiting on a transcript,
      not on a translation, and a button that names the wrong work makes a promise the
      click will not keep. -#}
  <p>{% if audio_waiting %}Not transcribed yet.{% else %}Not translated yet.{% endif %}</p>
  <button type="button" id="translate-chapter" hidden>{% if audio_waiting %}Transcribe{% else %}Translate{% endif %}</button>
</div>
{% endif %}
{#- The player. Shown on a dialogue the moment it opens, because a reader who does not
    know the audio is there will not go looking for it in a toolbar — and put away with the
    × for anyone who would rather read. The bar keeps its own button, which is how it comes
    back.

    In the corner rather than across the top: a bar over the text is a second toolbar,
    and it moves the reading down to make room for something a reader may never press. -#}
{% if spoken_audio %}
<div class="player" id="player" role="group" aria-label="Play {{ spoken_label }}">
  <button type="button" class="player-play" data-play-scene aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Play {{ spoken_label }}">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <path class="play" d="M5 3.5 12.5 8 5 12.5Z"></path>
      <rect class="stop" x="4.5" y="4.5" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect>
    </svg>
  </button>
  <span class="player-body">
    <span class="player-said">Listen to {{ spoken_label }}</span>
    <span class="player-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="player-fill"></span></span>
    <span class="player-clock" dir="ltr" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </span>
  {#- How fast. A pair rather than one button that cycles: a cycle from 2× comes round to
      0.5×, which is the one step nobody pressing "faster" means. In the card and not the
      bar, because the bar's button is only "play" and the speed is a fact about listening,
      not about the page. The readout is the typed × after a number (design.md §7). -#}
  <span class="player-rate" role="group" aria-label="Speed">
    <button type="button" class="player-slower" aria-label="Slower">−</button>
    <button type="button" class="player-rate-now" dir="ltr" aria-label="Speed" title="Speed">1×</button>
    <button type="button" class="player-faster" aria-label="Faster">+</button>
  </span>
  {#- A real file, carried in the page already, so saving it costs no request. -#}
  <a class="player-get" download aria-label="Download the audio" title="Download the audio">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <path d="M8 2.5v8"></path>
      <path d="M4.5 7.5 8 11l3.5-3.5"></path>
      <path d="M3 13.5h10"></path>
    </svg>
  </a>
  <button type="button" class="player-close" aria-label="Close the player">×</button>
</div>
{% endif %}
{#- The picture, in the same corner the player stands in, and away until asked for.
    The `<video>` is the page's one media element when it exists — the player strip
    drives it, and closing the panel only hides the picture, never stops the sound.
    The address is relative and the file stands beside this one: never inlined — a
    part of video is ten times the whole page — and never fetched from any network,
    so a folder that travels to a disk keeps its picture (design.md §12). -#}
{% if spoken_video %}
{#- data-src, not src: the page itself arrived with the serve key in its query, and a
    bare relative src would ask for the sidecar without it — refused, and the fallback
    would hide the whole feature. The script appends location.search, which is the key
    when there is one and nothing off a disk. -#}
<div class="video" id="video" hidden role="group" aria-label="Watch {{ spoken_label }}">
  <video class="video-el" playsinline preload="metadata" data-src="{{ spoken_video }}"></video>
  <button type="button" class="video-close" aria-label="Close the video">×</button>
</div>
{% endif %}
{#- A way to hear one line. Drawn once and called twice: a dialogue's turn and a verse of
    a recorded book are the same control over the same audio, and two copies of an inline
    SVG is how the two quietly stop matching. -#}
{%- macro hear(id) -%}
<button type="button" class="say" data-id="{{ id }}" aria-label="Play this line"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path class="play" d="M5 3.5 12.5 8 5 12.5Z"></path><rect class="stop" x="4.5" y="4.5" width="7" height="7" rx="1"></rect></svg></button>
{%- endmacro -%}
{% for segment in segments %}
  {% if segment.kind.value == 'heading' %}
  {% set level = segment.level|default(2, true) %}
  <div class="pair head{% if segment.id in pointed %} points{% endif %}{% if segment.id in unaccented %} accented{% endif %}{% if segment.id in machine %} machine{% endif %}" data-id="{{ segment.id }}" tabindex="-1">
    <h{{ level }} class="src plain" data-form="plain" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ bare[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</h{{ level }}>
    {% if segment.id in pointed %}
    <h{{ level }} class="src pointed" data-form="pointed" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ pointed[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</h{{ level }}>
    {% endif %}
    {% if segment.id in unaccented %}
    <h{{ level }} class="src unaccented" data-form="unaccented" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ unaccented[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</h{{ level }}>
    {% endif %}
    {% if translated %}<p class="tr" lang="{{ target_language }}" dir="{{ target_direction }}">{{ primary.get(segment.id, '')|isolate(target_direction) }}</p>{% endif %}
  </div>
  {% elif segment.kind.value == 'byline' %}
  <div class="pair byline{% if segment.id in pointed %} points{% endif %}{% if segment.id in unaccented %} accented{% endif %}{% if segment.id in machine %} machine{% endif %}" data-id="{{ segment.id }}" tabindex="-1">
    <p class="src plain" data-form="plain" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ bare[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% if segment.id in pointed %}
    <p class="src pointed" data-form="pointed" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ pointed[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    {% if segment.id in unaccented %}
    <p class="src unaccented" data-form="unaccented" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ unaccented[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    {% if translated %}<p class="tr" lang="{{ target_language }}" dir="{{ target_direction }}">{{ primary.get(segment.id, '')|isolate(target_direction) }}</p>{% endif %}
  </div>
  {% elif segment.kind.value == 'turn' %}
  {#- A line of a dialogue. Who said it sits beside the words rather than inside them, so
      the speaker is never pointed, never counted as vocabulary and never read aloud. The
      play control appears only where that line has a span of the scene's audio: a turn
      with no sound shows none, rather than a button that does nothing. -#}
  <div class="pair said{% if segment.id in spoken %} voiced{% endif %}{% if segment.id in pointed %} points{% endif %}{% if segment.id in unaccented %} accented{% endif %}{% if segment.id in primary_coarse %} coarse{% endif %}{% if segment.id in machine %} machine{% endif %}" data-id="{{ segment.id }}" data-speaker="{{ speakers.get(segment.id, '') }}" tabindex="-1">
    <span class="who" aria-hidden="true">{{ speakers.get(segment.id, '') }}</span>
    {% if segment.id in spoken %}{{ hear(segment.id) }}{% endif %}
    <p class="src plain" data-form="plain" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ bare[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% if segment.id in pointed %}
    <p class="src pointed" data-form="pointed" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ pointed[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    {% if segment.id in unaccented %}
    <p class="src unaccented" data-form="unaccented" lang="{{ source_language }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ unaccented[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    {% if translated %}<p class="tr" lang="{{ target_language }}" dir="{{ target_direction }}">{{ primary.get(segment.id, '')|isolate(target_direction) }}</p>{% endif %}
  </div>
  {% else %}
  {#- A verse answers to its address. The row is `#2:1`, which is what a link to Ruth 2:1
      says, and the number stands in the margin the way a printed edition sets it: a
      learner locates a line by chapter and verse and nothing else. The number is a link
      to itself, so the address bar carries the verse and a reader can hand it on. -#}
  {% set at = verses.get(segment.id, '') %}
  {#- A row in another language than the page's says so on its own cells: Daniel turns
      into Aramaic at 2:4 and back at 8:1, and `lang` is how a screen reader and a
      spell-checker are told. Where the language turns, a line above the row names it
      once, in the label's size and ink, so the switch is seen rather than inferred from
      words that have stopped answering to a tap. -#}
  {% set tongue = languages.get(segment.id, source_language) %}
  <div class="pair{% if at %} verse{% endif %}{% if segment.kind.value == 'blockquote' %} quote{% endif %}{% if segment.id in spoken %} voiced{% endif %}{% if segment.id in pointed %} points{% endif %}{% if segment.id in unaccented %} accented{% endif %}{% if segment.id in primary_coarse %} coarse{% endif %}{% if segment.id in machine %} machine{% endif %}{% if segment.id in languages %} unread{% endif %}"{% if at %} id="{{ at }}" data-ref="{{ segment.ref }}"{% endif %} data-id="{{ segment.id }}"{% if segment.id in languages %} data-lang="{{ tongue }}"{% endif %} tabindex="-1">
    {% if segment.id in switches %}<span class="language" lang="en">{{ switches[segment.id] }}</span>{% endif %}
    {% if at %}<a class="verse-number" href="#{{ at }}" aria-label="{{ segment.ref }}" title="{{ segment.ref }}">{{ at.split(':')[1] }}</a>{% endif %}
    {% if segment.id in spoken %}{{ hear(segment.id) }}{% endif %}
    <p class="src plain" data-form="plain" lang="{{ tongue }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ bare[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% if segment.id in pointed %}
    <p class="src pointed" data-form="pointed" lang="{{ tongue }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ pointed[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    {% if segment.id in unaccented %}
    <p class="src unaccented" data-form="unaccented" lang="{{ tongue }}" dir="{{ source_direction }}">{{ unaccented[segment.id]|isolate(source_direction) }}</p>
    {% endif %}
    {% if translated %}<p class="tr" lang="{{ target_language }}" dir="{{ target_direction }}">{{ primary.get(segment.id, '')|isolate(target_direction) }}</p>{% endif %}
  </div>
  {% endif %}
{% endfor %}
</main>

{#- Turning the page: back, where you are, forward. Fixed at the foot of the window in
    paged mode and not there at all otherwise. Arrows per reading direction, drawn by
    the stylesheet the way the pager's are. -#}
<div class="turn" id="turn" hidden>
  <button type="button" class="back" data-turn="-1" aria-label="Previous page"></button>
  <span id="page-of" dir="ltr"></span>
  <button type="button" class="forward" data-turn="1" aria-label="Next page"></button>
</div>

{#- At the foot, where a part ends: the words you never marked, offered as known in one
    press, with one Undo. Offered, never done for you — and never a name, which does not
    count. "Words should be marked as known automatically when I'm done with the
    article", said the first alpha reader; this is the honest version of that. -#}
{#- Both strips are English chrome inside a page that may run right to left: without
    their own direction the "?" wandered to the front and "2 of 4" read "of 4 2". -#}
{% if words %}
<div class="rest" id="rest" dir="ltr" hidden>
  <span id="rest-text"></span>
  <button type="button" id="rest-mark"></button>
  <button type="button" id="rest-undo" hidden>Undo</button>
</div>
{% endif %}
{#- Finished with the text: one press at the foot of its last part, and a line that says
    when. Pressing again takes it back, so however often it is pressed the text is
    finished once. Counted on the progress page. -#}
{% if not following %}
<div class="finished" id="finished" dir="ltr">
  <span id="done-said" hidden></span>
  <button type="button" id="done-mark">Done</button>
</div>
{#- And where to go next. Hidden until the script says the page is being served: the
    address is the library's, and a reader opened from a disk has no library to go to. -#}
{% if suggested %}
<aside class="next-up" id="next-up" dir="ltr" hidden>
  <span class="next-up-lead">Read next{% if suggested.scene %} · {{ suggested.scene }}{% endif %}</span>
  {#- `/library#id`, which is where Learn sends a reader for the same reason: the library
      marks the row and scrolls to it, and lifts a filter that would have hidden it.
      `/library/<id>` is a different page — the public one, off unless the shelves are —
      and linking there gave a signed-in reader "not found". -#}
  <a class="next-up-link" href="/library#{{ suggested.id }}">{{ suggested.title }}{% if suggested.english %} <span class="next-up-english" lang="en">· {{ suggested.english }}</span>{% endif %}</a>
  {% if suggested.blurb %}<span class="next-up-why">{{ suggested.blurb }}</span>{% endif %}
</aside>
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

{% if not standalone %}
<nav class="pager" data-chapter="{{ section.number }}" data-document="{{ document.content_hash }}">
  {% if previous %}<a class="prev" href="{{ previous.filename }}">{{ previous.title }}</a>{% endif %}
  {% if following %}<a class="next" href="{{ following.filename }}" data-next="{{ following.number }}">{{ following.title }}</a>{% endif %}
</nav>
{% endif %}

{% if words %}
<aside class="list" id="list" aria-label="What you have kept from this text">
  <div class="list-head">
    <div class="list-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Words or phrases">
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-words" data-list="words" aria-selected="true"
              aria-controls="list-items"><b id="list-count">0</b> <span id="list-count-label">words</span></button>
      <button type="button" role="tab" id="tab-phrases" data-list="phrases" aria-selected="false"
              aria-controls="phrase-items"><b id="phrase-count">0</b> <span id="phrase-count-label">phrases</span></button>
    </div>
    <button type="button" class="list-close" data-toggle="list" aria-label="Hide the list">×</button>
  </div>
  <p class="list-stats" id="list-stats"></p>
  {#- "Mark them in the text" was here, and it took the colours off and left everything
      else on. The Mark button in the bar does the whole job now. -#}
  <div class="list-body">
    <ol class="list-items" id="list-items" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-words"></ol>
    <p class="list-empty" id="words-empty">Tap any word to say how well you know it.</p>
    <ol class="list-items" id="phrase-items" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-phrases" hidden></ol>
    <p class="list-empty" id="phrases-empty" hidden>Drag across words to keep a phrase.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="list-foot">
    <button type="button" data-export="csv" id="export-button">Export</button>
    {#- The same list again, shaped for Anki: the pointed word on the front, the sentence
        it was met in on the back. A file most learners already have somewhere to put. -#}
    <button type="button" data-export="anki" id="anki-button">Anki deck</button>
  </div>
</aside>
<button type="button" class="list-tab" id="list-tab" data-toggle="list" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="list"><b id="list-tab-count">0</b> <span id="list-tab-label">words</span><span id="list-tab-phrases" hidden></span></button>
{% endif %}

<div id="pick-chip" class="pick-card" hidden></div>
{% if words %}<div id="gloss-card" class="gloss-card" hidden></div>{% endif %}
{#- Before the scripts, not after them. `reader.js` asks for this by id as it loads, and
    for as long as it sat below the script tag the answer was null: the `?` key and the `?`
    button in the bar both went to a card that was never found, so the one page that says
    which keys exist could not be opened by either. -#}
<div class="keys-card" id="keys" hidden role="dialog" aria-label="Keyboard shortcuts">
  {#- Named and closable where a pointer can see it. Escape and a click elsewhere both
      put it away, but nothing on the card said so, and the list beside the text already
      closes with the typed × the guidelines ask for. -#}
  <div class="keys-head"><b>Keys</b><button type="button" class="keys-close" data-keys aria-label="Close">×</button></div>
  {#- In the order a reader reaches for them, not by category: the walk and the levels are
      most of a session, the modes are set once and left. `t` is not here — it reports how
      long the page took to draw, which is for whoever is building targum rather than for
      whoever is reading. -#}
  <dl>
    {% if words %}<dt>&larr; &rarr;</dt><dd>forward through the words you have not finished with, then the foot of the page, then the next page — and, off the last, the next chapter; back through the words as they are written</dd>
    <dt>k 4</dt><dd>known</dd>
    <dt>1 2 3</dt><dd>just met it, getting there, nearly know it</dd>
    <dt>i</dt><dd>ignore it — a name, a numeral, a word from another language</dd>
    <dt>Enter</dt><dd>what the word you are on means; again to look it up</dd>
    <dt>u</dt><dd>take back the last level, and stand on that word again</dd>
    <dt>Esc</dt><dd>close this, then a word, then your saved words, then the queue</dd>
    <dt>?</dt><dd>these keys</dd>
    <dt>hard</dt><dd>the level on a word's card says how common it is across the language; the number in the library says how much of a text will be new to you</dd>
    <dt>s</dt><dd>your saved words</dd>
    <dt>m</dt><dd>mark words as you read</dd>
    <dt>b</dt><dd>pages, or one long scroll</dd>
    <dt>f</dt><dd>full screen, and out of it</dd>
    {% if spoken_audio %}<dt>Space</dt><dd>play {{ spoken_label }}, and pause it where it is</dd>
    <dt>&lt; &gt;</dt><dd>slower, faster</dd>{% endif %}
    <dt>PageDown</dt><dd>next page; PageUp back. On the last page, the next chapter</dd>
    {% else %}<dt>Esc</dt><dd>close this</dd>
    <dt>?</dt><dd>these keys</dd>{% endif %}
    {% if has_nikkud %}<dt>n</dt><dd>vowel points</dd>{% endif %}
    {% if has_taamim %}<dt>a</dt><dd>the chanting marks, with the vowels on</dd>{% endif %}
    <dt>p</dt><dd>parallel</dd>
    <dt>l</dt><dd>interlinear — the translation under each line</dd>
    <dt>o</dt><dd>source only</dd>
  </dl>
  {#- Who read it. A recording the library serves is somebody else's work under a licence
      that asks for them to be named, and naming them in a file nobody opens is not
      naming them. The text's own credit is on the library page; this one travels with
      the audio, because the audio is what can be saved and carried off. -#}
  {% if speech_credit %}
  <p class="keys-credit">Read by {{ speech_credit }}{% if speech_licence %} · {% if speech_licence_url %}<a href="{{ speech_licence_url }}" rel="noopener">{{ speech_licence }}</a>{% else %}{{ speech_licence }}{% endif %}{% endif %}</p>
  {% endif %}
  {#- Said once, here, rather than left for a reader to work out from a wrong card.
      The lemmatiser is trained on modern unpointed Hebrew, so on the Tanakh it is
      guessing at forms the training data barely contains: waw-consecutive, pausal
      forms, archaic suffixes. On a modern text this does not need saying, so it is
      not said. targum-internal #87, which asked for the about page — that page is a
      construction notice now, and the sentence belongs where the words are anyway. -#}
  {#- What read the words, named because its licence asks to be. CC BY 4.0 wants the
      work named and the licence linked wherever the work is used, and a credit in a
      file nobody opens is the same non-naming the reading credit above refuses. -#}
  {% if words_credit %}
  <p class="keys-credit">Dictionary forms by <a href="https://huggingface.co/dicta-il/dictabert-joint" rel="noopener">DICTA</a> · <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="noopener">CC BY 4.0</a></p>
  {% endif %}
  {% if biblical %}
  <p class="keys-credit">Dictionary forms on biblical Hebrew are less reliable than on
    modern: the analyser is trained on modern unpointed text, so waw-consecutive, pausal
    and archaic forms are sometimes read wrongly on a card.</p>
  {% endif %}
</div>
<script type="application/json" id="targum-data">{{ data }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('vocab.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('sync.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('reader.js') }}</script>
</body>
</html>
